sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: To bisect a given finite straight line.
----
proof:

Let {AB} be the given finite straight line.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="240" height="200" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="I.10 diagram" src="propI10.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
geomlib.init({
    canvasid: "canvas_0",
    background: "35,19,100",
    title: "I.10",
    pivot: "D",
    elements: [
        "A;point;free;40,170",
        "B;point;free;200,170",
        "AB;line;connect;A,B",
        "ABC;polygon;equilateralTriangle;A,B;0;0;black;random",
        "C;point;vertex;ABC,3",
        "D;point;midpoint;A,B;black;green",
        "CD;line;connect;C,D",
        // Transitory construction — real-colored, but listed in
        // initiallyHidden below (geomlib 0.9.0) so they stay out of the
        // static figure and appear only on the build/bisect slides: the
        // two equilateral-triangle circles, the lower intersection E,
        // and the second triangle's sides (the I.9 double-triangle
        // bisection). The Guide canvas runs the same construction but
        // keeps its circles; here they vanish, returning to △ABC + CD.
        "Acirc;circle;radius;A,B;0;0;black;0",
        "Bcirc;circle;radius;B,A;0;0;black;0",
        "ABbi;line;bichord;Acirc,Bcirc;0;0;0",
        "E;point;first;ABbi;black;green",
        "AE;line;connect;A,E;0;0;black",
        "EB;line;connect;E,B;0;0;black",
        // Bisector drawn C → E on the bisect slide (through D to the
        // lower apex); transitory (initiallyHidden) — it settles to the
        // persistent CD on the next slide, Joyce's figure.
        "CE;line;connect;C,E;0;0;black",
        // Zero-color highlight targets for the prose's {NAME} refs.
        "CA;line;connect;C,A;0;0;0",
        "CB;line;connect;C,B;0;0;0",
        "AD;line;connect;A,D;0;0;0",
        "BD;line;connect;B,D;0;0;0",
        // Angle markers at C (0.8.0 angleMarker): the whole angle ACB
        // and the two halves ACD/BCD. 3 markers at C overlap at the
        // default radius until 0.8.1 auto-nesting (deck-tracker.md).
        "angACB;sector;angleMarker;C,A,B",
        "angACD;sector;angleMarker;C,A,D",
        "angBCD;sector;angleMarker;C,B,D"
    ],
    // Real-colored construction scaffolding kept out of the static
    // figure (0.9.0). Angle markers auto-hide; these need listing.
    initiallyHidden: ["Acirc","Bcirc","E","AE","EB","CE"],
    aliases: {
        "ACB": "angACB",
        "ACD": "angACD",
        "BCD": "angBCD"
    },
    slides: [
        { text: "Let {AB} be the given finite straight line.",
          visible: ["AB"] },
        { text: "It is required to bisect the finite straight line {AB}.",
          visible: ["AB"],
          highlighted: ["AB"] },
        { text: "Construct the equilateral triangle {ABC} on it,",
          visible: ["AB","Acirc","Bcirc","C","ABC"],
          transition: {
              animations: [
                  { elem: "Acirc", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass },
                  { elem: "Bcirc", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass },
                  { elem: "C",     name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "ABC",   name: geomlib.A.Polygon.outlineAndFill }
              ]
          },
          justifications: [{ ref: "I.1" }] },
        { text: "and bisect the angle {ACB} by the straight line {CD}.",
          visible: ["AB","Acirc","Bcirc","C","ABC","angACB","E","AE","EB","CE","D","angACD","angBCD"],
          transition: {
              animations: [
                  { elem: "angACB", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "E",      name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "AE",     name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "EB",     name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "CE",     name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "D",      name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "angACD", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angBCD", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep }
              ]
          },
          justifications: [{ ref: "I.9" }] },
        { text: "I say that the straight line {AB} is bisected at the point {D}.",
          visible: ["AB","ABC","C","CD","D"],
          highlighted: ["AB","D"] },
        { text: "Since {CA} equals {CB}, and {CD} is common, therefore the two sides {CA} and {CD} equal the two sides {CB} and {CD} respectively, and the angle {ACD} equals the angle {BCD},",
          visible: ["AB","ABC","C","CD","D","CA","CB","angACD","angBCD"],
          highlighted: ["CA","CB","CD","angACD","angBCD"] },
        { text: "therefore the base {AD} equals the base {BD}.",
          visible: ["AB","ABC","C","CD","D","CA","CB","AD","BD"],
          highlighted: ["AD","BD"],
          justifications: [{ ref: "I.Def.20" }, { ref: "I.4" }] },
        { text: "Therefore the given straight line {AB} is bisected at {D}.",
          visible: ["AB","ABC","C","CD","D","AD","BD"],
          highlighted: ["AD","BD","D"],
          justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.F." }] }
    ],
    resolveJustification: function(ref) {
        return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
    }
});

// Per-page citation lookup shared by both figures' resolveJustification
// (window.eucrefs is global; canvas_1's callback reads it too).
window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
    "I.1":       "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI1/",
    "I.4":       "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI4/",
    "I.9":       "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI9/",
    "I.Def.20":  "/elements/books/bookI/definitions/defI20/"
});
</script>
</figure>

It is required to bisect the finite straight line {AB}.

Construct the equilateral triangle {ABC} on it, and bisect the angle {ACB} by the straight line {CD}. [!just I.1, I.9]

I say that the straight line {AB} is bisected at the point {D}.

Since {CA} equals {CB}, and {CD} is common, therefore the two sides {CA} and {CD} equal the two sides {CB} and {CD} respectively, and the angle {ACD} equals the angle {BCD}, therefore the base {AD} equals the base {BD}. [!just I.Def.20; I.4]

Therefore the given straight line {AB} is bisected at {D}.

<div class="qed">Q.E.F.</div>
<br clear="all">
----
guide:

While this construction divides a line into two equal parts, the construction in proposition @VI.9 divides a line into any given number of equal parts.

#### Construction steps

<figure class="diagram rdiagram">
<canvas id="canvas_1" width="353" height="257" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="I.10 construction steps" src="propI10a.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
geomlib.init({
    canvasid: "canvas_1",
    background: "0,0,100",
    title: "I.10",
    elements: [
        "A;point;free;121,128",
        "B;point;free;231,128",
        "AB;line;connect;A,B",
        "Acirc;circle;radius;A,B;0;0;black;300,30,100",
        "Bcirc;circle;radius;B,A;0;0;black;300,30,100",
        "CEbi;line;bichord;Acirc,Bcirc;0;0;0",
        "E;point;first;CEbi",
        "C;point;last;CEbi",
        "D;point;intersection;A,B,C,E",
        // Visible bisector drawn C → E (top-down) — a connect line so the
        // straightEdge stroke runs from C to E; the bichord above is kept
        // only as a hidden helper to derive the C and E intersections.
        "CE;line;connect;C,E",
        // Illustrative triangle sides + the two halves (zero-color;
        // Joyce: these "aren't necessary ... only used to show the
        // construction is correct"). Shown gold on their slides,
        // invisible in the static figure (Joyce's two circles + CE).
        "AC;line;connect;A,C;0;0;0",
        "CB;line;connect;C,B;0;0;0",
        "AE;line;connect;A,E;0;0;0",
        "EB;line;connect;E,B;0;0;0",
        "AD;line;connect;A,D;0;0;0",
        "BD;line;connect;B,D;0;0;0",
        // The angle being bisected (shows in the static figure — Q2).
        "angACB;sector;angleMarker;C,A,B"
    ],
    aliases: {
        "BA":  "AB",
        "BC":  "CB",
        "ACB": "angACB"
    },
    slides: [
        { text: "This method for bisecting lines takes less actual work than it appears to. It is really no more than the double-equilateral-triangle.",
          visible: ["AB"] },
        { text: "First, the equilateral triangle ABC needs to be constructed. According to I.1 two circles need to be drawn: one with center {A} and radius {AB}, the other with center {B} and radius {BA}.",
          visible: ["AB","Acirc","Bcirc"],
          transition: {
              mode: "parallel",
              animations: [
                  { elem: "Acirc", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass },
                  { elem: "Bcirc", name: geomlib.A.Circle.compass }
              ]
          },
          justifications: [{ ref: "I.1" }] },
        { text: "One of the points of intersection of the two circles is {C}.",
          visible: ["AB","Acirc","Bcirc","C"],
          highlighted: ["AC","CB"],
          transition: {
              animations: [
                  { elem: "C",  name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "AC", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "CB", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect }
              ]
          } },
        { text: "Then to bisect angle {ACB}, according to I.9, an arbitrary point is chosen on one side of the angle, and it might as well be the point {A} on the side {AC}, and a point equally far from {C} on the side {BC}, which is, of course, {B}.",
          visible: ["AB","Acirc","Bcirc","C"],
          highlighted: ["AC","CB","angACB"],
          transition: {
              animations: [
                  { elem: "angACB", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep }
              ]
          },
          justifications: [{ ref: "I.9" }] },
        { text: "Then an equilateral triangle is constructed on the line {AB}. There are two such equilateral triangles, the one already constructed ACB, and another one, call it AEB.",
          visible: ["AB","Acirc","Bcirc","C","E"],
          highlighted: ["AE","EB"],
          transition: {
              animations: [
                  { elem: "E",  name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "AE", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "EB", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect }
              ]
          } },
        { text: "The point {E} is the other intersection of the two circles already drawn.",
          visible: ["AB","Acirc","Bcirc","C","E"],
          highlighted: ["E"] },
        { text: "Then, by I.9, the line {CE} bisects the angle {ACB},",
          visible: ["AB","Acirc","Bcirc","C","E","CE"],
          highlighted: ["CE","angACB","AC","CB"],
          transition: {
              animations: [
                  { elem: "CE", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect }
              ]
          },
          justifications: [{ ref: "I.9" }] },
        { text: "and according to this proposition, the point {D} bisects the line {AB}.",
          visible: ["AB","Acirc","Bcirc","C","E","CE","D"],
          highlighted: ["D","AD","BD"],
          transition: {
              animations: [
                  { elem: "D", name: geomlib.A.Point.appear }
              ]
          } },
        { text: "Actually, only two circles and the straight line {CE} need to be drawn. The straight lines {AC}, {CB}, {AE}, and {EB}, aren't necessary for the construction; they are only used to show that the construction is correct.",
          visible: ["AB","Acirc","Bcirc","C","E","CE","D"],
          highlighted: ["AC","CB","AE","EB"] }
    ],
    resolveJustification: function(ref) {
        return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
    }
});
</script>
</figure>

This method for bisecting lines takes less actual work than it appears to. It is really no more than the double-equilateral-triangle.

First, the equilateral triangle *ABC* needs to be constructed. According to @I.1 two circles need to be drawn: one with center {A} and radius {AB}, the other with center {B} and radius {BA}. One of the points of intersection of the two circles is {C}. Then to bisect angle {ACB}, according to @I.9, an arbitrary point is chosen on one side of the angle, and it might as well be the point {A} on the side {AC}, and a point equally far from {C} on the side {BC}, which is, of course, {B}. Then an equilateral triangle is constructed on the line {AB}. There are two such equilateral triangles, the one already constructed *ACB*, and another one, call it *AEB*. The point {E} is the other intersection of the two circles already drawn. Then, by I.9, the line {CE} bisects the angle {ACB}, and according to this proposition, the point {D} bisects the line {AB}.

Actually, only two circles and the straight line {CE} need to be drawn. The straight lines {AC}, {CB}, {AE}, and {EB}, aren't necessary for the construction; they are only used to show that the construction is correct.

#### Use of Proposition 10

The construction of this proposition in Book I is used in propositions @I.12, @I.16, and @I.42. It is also used in several propositions in the Books II, III, IV, X, and XIII.
